@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# MAME Tauri Post-Closeout Hardening Spec — 2026-09-13
+
+**Repository:** `ekkus93/mame`  
+**Baseline:** post-MT-2200 `master` after engineering-phase closure  
+**Scope:** hardening issues found in the post-closeout review of the production-useful external-window Tauri frontend
+
+## 1. Purpose
+
+MT-2200 closed the required engineering phase for the external-window Tauri frontend. This post-closeout hardening batch does not reopen that closure. It addresses review findings that improve user-facing completeness, state freshness, security documentation, generator maintainability, and frontend/backend type fidelity.
+
+## 2. Non-goals
+
+This batch must not reactivate the deferred optional research tracks:
+
+- MT-1000 native-window / embedded-rendering research;
+- MT-1100 dedicated Tauri MAME OSD;
+- MT-1200 in-process MAME hosting;
+- MT-1705 embedded-render performance qualification.
+
+No video frame, PCM stream, gameplay-input stream, or generic shell/filesystem privilege should be moved through Tauri IPC.
+
+## 3. Requirements
+
+### PCH-001 — Visible runtime controls
+
+The session panel must expose the runtime-control protocol that already exists at the Rust and frontend command layers:
+
+- pause;
+- resume;
+- soft reset;
+- mute/unmute;
+- bounded runtime-state refresh/query;
+- stop remains visible and bounded.
+
+Controls must be disabled unless a supervised MAME session is in the `running` state. The UI must present bounded status text and must surface backend errors through the existing error envelope path.
+
+### PCH-002 — Fresh keyboard/gameplay ownership state
+
+The library keyboard shortcut guard must clear stale gameplay-input ownership when a supervised session exits, crashes, or fails. It may do so by direct lifecycle subscription or by triggering the existing focus-time ownership refresh from the authoritative session lifecycle UI. It must remain fail-closed while ownership cannot be queried, and shortcuts must still be suppressed while MAME owns gameplay input or the Tauri document lacks focus.
+
+### PCH-003 — Runtime-control bootstrap file contract
+
+The private runtime-control Lua bootstrap contains a per-session bearer token and must remain ephemeral and non-shared.
+
+Unix builds must retain `0600` permissions. Windows and other non-Unix builds currently rely on the temporary-file and per-user temporary-directory semantics available through the Rust standard library and the host OS. The project documentation and regression tests must keep that contract explicit: the path must remain ephemeral, random, process-owned, and RAII-deleted, and it must not be changed to a shared, predictable, or persistent script path without adding an explicit Windows ACL implementation.
+
+### PCH-004 — Runtime-control composition guard
+
+The runtime-control source-generation path must remain fail-closed. Until it is fully converted away from anchor replacement, the hardening batch must add a regression guard that detects loss of anchor-drift assertions, generated-source wiring, or fragment rerun tracking.
+
+### PCH-005 — Frontend session payload fidelity
+
+The TypeScript `SessionSnapshot` type must match the richer Rust payload used by lifecycle events and diagnostics. The frontend may render only a subset, but the type must not hide executable identity, effective argv/config, timestamps, exit status, output tails, truncation flags, or diagnostic error state.
+
+### PCH-006 — CI hardening regression
+
+The hardening batch must add a checked-in static regression that verifies the visible UI controls, lifecycle-event keyboard ownership fix, bootstrap contract, composition invariants, and richer `SessionSnapshot` typing remain present. The Tauri project workflow must run this regression before the normal frontend/Rust build and test sequence.
+
+### PCH-007 — Qualification protocol
+
+The batch must be qualified through the same exact-head discipline used by MT-2200: green branch CI before promotion, guarded PR merge, and post-merge `master` CI inspection on the promoted SHA. Exact run IDs should be treated as external CI evidence rather than self-referential content inside the candidate commit.
+
+## 4. Acceptance criteria
+
+The batch is accepted when:
+
+1. the visible session UI exposes pause/resume/reset/mute/query controls;
+2. library keyboard ownership updates through lifecycle-triggered refresh notification as well as focus-time polling;
+3. the non-Unix runtime-control bootstrap security contract is explicit in documentation and regression-checked;
+4. runtime-control composition invariants are regression-checked;
+5. frontend `SessionSnapshot` typing covers the Rust fields;
+6. the post-closeout TODO is fully reconciled with no ambiguous unchecked required items;
+7. exact-head CI is green before promotion.
+
+## 5. Validation plan
+
+Local/static validation:
+
+- `python3 scripts/tauri/test-post-closeout-hardening.py`;
+- existing checked-in Python helper tests where the sandbox supports them.
+
+Repository CI validation:
+
+- `Tauri project` must pass and covers frontend format, lint, typecheck, tests, Rust format/tests/Clippy, and the static hardening regression;
+- `Tauri security` must pass and covers the privileged boundary plus the bootstrap-file security contract;
+- package/documentation workflows should remain green for promoted changes.
